 one at a time, trying every colour not used by a neighbour.","Backtrack as soon as a node has no legal colour left."],"complexity":"O(m^n) worst case, O(n) space","starter":{"python":"def graphColoring(n, edges, m):\n    # TODO: implement\n    pass\n","javascript":"/**\n * @param {*} n\n * @param {*} edges\n * @param {*} m\n * @return {*}\n */\nvar graphColoring = function (n, edges, m) {\n  // TODO: implement\n};\n"},"editorial":{"explanation":"Colour nodes one at a time; for each, try every colour not used by an already-coloured neighbour,\nrecurse, and reset the colour on failure.\n\nFailing early matters: a node with no legal colour aborts the branch immediately.\n\nWorst case `O(m^n)`, which is why `n` is small. Ordering nodes by degree first prunes far more in\npractice.","code":{"python":"def graphColoring(n, edges, m):\n    adj = [set() for _ in range(n)]\n    for a, b in edges:\n        adj[a].add(b)\n        adj[b].add(a)\n    colour = [0] * n\n\n    def walk(node):\n        if node == n:\n            return True\n        for c in range(1, m + 1):\n            if all(colour[neighbour] != c for neighbour in adj[node]):\n                colour[node] = c\n                if walk(node + 1):\n                    return True\n                colour[node] = 0\n        return False\n\n    return walk(0)\n"},"complexity":"O(m^n) worst case, O(n) space"}}
